1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,818
This video has three parts.

2
00:00:01,918 --> 00:00:05,208
First, the physics of restrained wellhead growth.

3
00:00:05,308 --> 00:00:10,052
Second, a side by side comparison of a free and a restrained wellhead.

4
00:00:10,152 --> 00:00:12,612
And third, answers to your questions.

5
00:00:12,712 --> 00:00:13,234
Part one.

6
00:00:13,334 --> 00:00:14,065
The physics.

7
00:00:15,983 --> 00:00:18,670
When a subsea production well starts flowing,

8
00:00:18,770 --> 00:00:22,509
hot oil from the reservoir warms every steel string around it,

9
00:00:22,609 --> 00:00:24,614
and the steel wants to get longer.

10
00:00:24,714 --> 00:00:27,152
Normally the wellhead is allowed to move.

11
00:00:27,252 --> 00:00:29,753
It rises a few centimetres off the seabed.

12
00:00:30,317 --> 00:00:31,762
But what if you don't let it?

13
00:00:31,862 --> 00:00:35,813
What if the wellhead and the conductor are held, so they cannot move upward?

14
00:00:35,913 --> 00:00:37,305
Where does the expansion go?

15
00:00:37,405 --> 00:00:38,744
And does the casing buckle?

16
00:00:39,317 --> 00:00:42,438
In this video we build a well with standard casing sizes,

17
00:00:42,538 --> 00:00:47,016
apply the production temperature, and follow the physics all the way to the loads

18
00:00:47,116 --> 00:00:47,977
and the buckling.

19
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:50,506
Here is our example.

20
00:00:50,606 --> 00:00:54,213
An oil producer in three hundred and fifty metres of water.

21
00:00:54,313 --> 00:00:57,228
The thirty inch conductor, with a one inch wall,

22
00:00:57,328 --> 00:01:00,621
is drilled and cemented eighty metres into the seabed.

23
00:01:00,721 --> 00:01:03,448
It carries the low pressure wellhead housing.

24
00:01:03,983 --> 00:01:08,496
The twenty inch surface casing, one hundred and thirty three pounds per foot,

25
00:01:08,596 --> 00:01:13,049
runs to seven hundred metres and is cemented all the way back to the seabed.

26
00:01:13,149 --> 00:01:17,601
On top of it sits the eighteen and three quarter inch high pressure wellhead

27
00:01:17,701 --> 00:01:21,196
housing, which lands and locks inside the conductor housing.

28
00:01:21,717 --> 00:01:25,805
Next comes the thirteen and three eighths inch intermediate casing,

29
00:01:25,905 --> 00:01:30,556
seventy two pounds per foot, grade L eighty, set at eighteen hundred metres.

30
00:01:30,656 --> 00:01:33,620
Only the bottom five hundred metres are cemented.

31
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:38,246
Above the top of cement, thirteen hundred metres of pipe hang free in mud.

32
00:01:38,783 --> 00:01:41,901
Then the nine and five eighths inch production casing,

33
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:45,239
fifty three and a half pounds per foot, grade P one ten,

34
00:01:45,339 --> 00:01:48,278
set at three thousand two hundred and fifty metres,

35
00:01:48,378 --> 00:01:51,973
with its top of cement at two thousand five hundred and fifty.

36
00:01:52,073 --> 00:01:56,323
A seven inch liner covers the reservoir, and five and a half inch tubing,

37
00:01:56,423 --> 00:01:58,945
with a production packer, brings the oil up.

38
00:01:59,483 --> 00:02:02,187
The spaces between the strings are the annuli.

39
00:02:02,287 --> 00:02:05,965
The A annulus is between the tubing and the production casing.

40
00:02:06,065 --> 00:02:08,342
B is outside the nine and five eighths,

41
00:02:08,442 --> 00:02:11,267
and C is outside the thirteen and three eighths.

42
00:02:11,367 --> 00:02:14,619
Each top of cement sits below the previous casing shoe,

43
00:02:14,719 --> 00:02:19,677
so the B and C annuli can bleed into the formation instead of building up pressure.

44
00:02:20,217 --> 00:02:24,916
The key feature for this problem is the free, uncemented length of each string.

45
00:02:25,016 --> 00:02:27,406
Each one is a long, slender steel column,

46
00:02:27,506 --> 00:02:31,537
fixed in cement at the bottom and hung from the wellhead at the top.

47
00:02:32,883 --> 00:02:34,653
Let's zoom in on the wellhead.

48
00:02:34,753 --> 00:02:39,576
Each casing string hangs from a hanger that lands on a load shoulder inside the

49
00:02:39,676 --> 00:02:40,948
high pressure housing.

50
00:02:41,048 --> 00:02:44,251
A seal assembly and a lockdown ring hold it in place.

51
00:02:44,351 --> 00:02:46,868
So every string is tied to the same point.

52
00:02:47,417 --> 00:02:51,926
Downward loads, like hanging weight, travel through the hangers into the housing,

53
00:02:52,026 --> 00:02:55,284
then into the cemented twenty inch and thirty inch strings,

54
00:02:55,384 --> 00:02:56,763
and finally into the soil.

55
00:02:57,283 --> 00:03:02,080
If a hot string pushes upward instead, its hanger pushes up against the lockdown,

56
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:05,587
and that force must be carried by the very same structure.

57
00:03:06,917 --> 00:03:08,151
Now, the temperature.

58
00:03:08,251 --> 00:03:13,042
Before production, everything sits at the undisturbed geothermal temperature.

59
00:03:13,142 --> 00:03:18,060
Six degrees at the seabed, rising three point three degrees per hundred metres,

60
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:21,808
to about one hundred and eighteen degrees at the reservoir.

61
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:26,534
This is also the temperature at which the cement set around each casing.

62
00:03:26,634 --> 00:03:28,974
So it is the stress free reference state.

63
00:03:29,074 --> 00:03:33,734
Every load in this problem comes from the change in temperature from this state.

64
00:03:34,883 --> 00:03:39,545
In production, about three thousand cubic metres of liquid per day flows up the

65
00:03:39,645 --> 00:03:39,967
tubing.

66
00:03:40,067 --> 00:03:43,884
It moves so fast that it loses only about ten degrees on the way,

67
00:03:43,984 --> 00:03:47,923
and arrives at the wellhead at about one hundred and eight degrees.

68
00:03:48,023 --> 00:03:52,744
The gap between the flowing temperature and the rock is largest near the seabed,

69
00:03:52,844 --> 00:03:54,311
where the rock is coldest.

70
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:59,727
That heat flows radially outward, through every layer, to the cold formation.

71
00:03:59,827 --> 00:04:03,669
Each layer is a thermal resistance, like resistors in series.

72
00:04:03,769 --> 00:04:06,254
The formation itself is the largest one,

73
00:04:06,354 --> 00:04:10,973
so the casings end up much closer to the oil temperature than to the rock

74
00:04:11,073 --> 00:04:11,748
temperature.

75
00:04:12,283 --> 00:04:15,237
Just below the seabed, after a year of production,

76
00:04:15,337 --> 00:04:18,352
the tubing has warmed by about one hundred degrees,

77
00:04:18,452 --> 00:04:20,917
the nine and five eighths by ninety seven,

78
00:04:21,017 --> 00:04:25,803
the thirteen and three eighths by eighty seven, the twenty inch by seventy five,

79
00:04:25,903 --> 00:04:27,819
and the conductor by about sixty.

80
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:32,386
Deeper down the warming fades, and it reaches zero at the reservoir.

81
00:04:32,486 --> 00:04:34,758
The inner strings respond within hours.

82
00:04:34,858 --> 00:04:38,285
The outer strings and the rock warm over weeks and months,

83
00:04:38,385 --> 00:04:41,326
so the loads keep creeping up long after start up.

84
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:48,329
Steel expands by about twelve point four millionths of its length for every degree.

85
00:04:48,429 --> 00:04:50,488
Integrate that over the free length.

86
00:04:50,588 --> 00:04:55,107
The thirteen and three eighths, warmed by seventy one degrees on average over

87
00:04:55,207 --> 00:04:59,785
thirteen hundred metres, would grow one point one four metres if it were free.

88
00:04:59,885 --> 00:05:03,084
The nine and five eighths would grow almost two metres.

89
00:05:03,617 --> 00:05:05,297
But the string is not free.

90
00:05:05,397 --> 00:05:09,911
The bottom is locked in cement, and the top is locked in the wellhead.

91
00:05:10,011 --> 00:05:15,251
If the length cannot change, the thermal strain must be cancelled by an equal and

92
00:05:15,351 --> 00:05:17,031
opposite mechanical strain.

93
00:05:17,131 --> 00:05:19,339
And mechanical strain means stress.

94
00:05:19,439 --> 00:05:22,173
Sigma equals E, times alpha, times delta T.

95
00:05:22,717 --> 00:05:27,669
For steel, that is about two and a half megapascals of compression for every degree.

96
00:05:27,769 --> 00:05:32,481
Multiply by the steel area, and every degree adds thirty four kilonewtons in the

97
00:05:32,581 --> 00:05:36,812
thirteen and three eighths, and twenty six in the nine and five eighths.

98
00:05:36,912 --> 00:05:41,142
Fully restrained, that is two point four, and one point six meganewtons.

99
00:05:42,483 --> 00:05:46,044
So how much does the wellhead actually move in a normal well?

100
00:05:46,144 --> 00:05:48,024
Think of each string as a spring.

101
00:05:48,124 --> 00:05:50,184
A long free casing is a soft spring.

102
00:05:50,284 --> 00:05:55,105
The thirteen and three eighths has an axial stiffness of about two meganewtons per

103
00:05:55,205 --> 00:05:55,465
metre.

104
00:05:55,565 --> 00:05:59,905
The conductor and the cemented surface casing, gripped by cement and soil,

105
00:06:00,005 --> 00:06:02,186
are more than a hundred times stiffer.

106
00:06:02,717 --> 00:06:07,784
The hot inner strings push up on the wellhead with about four meganewtons in total.

107
00:06:07,884 --> 00:06:09,963
The stiff foundation hardly yields.

108
00:06:10,063 --> 00:06:14,507
At the same time, the conductor and the surface casing expand themselves,

109
00:06:14,607 --> 00:06:17,060
sliding in the soft soil near the seabed.

110
00:06:17,583 --> 00:06:21,821
Solve for equilibrium, and the wellhead grows about sixty one millimetres.

111
00:06:21,921 --> 00:06:25,398
Forty four of those come from the foundation's own expansion,

112
00:06:25,498 --> 00:06:28,270
and seventeen from the push of the inner strings.

113
00:06:28,817 --> 00:06:30,667
And here is the surprising part.

114
00:06:30,767 --> 00:06:35,358
Sixty one millimetres is tiny compared with the one point one four metres the

115
00:06:35,458 --> 00:06:37,430
thirteen and three eighths wanted.

116
00:06:37,530 --> 00:06:42,488
So even in a normal subsea well, the inner strings are already ninety to ninety six

117
00:06:42,588 --> 00:06:43,645
percent restrained.

118
00:06:43,745 --> 00:06:46,997
They already carry almost the full thermal compression.

119
00:06:48,317 --> 00:06:51,971
Now let's hold the wellhead and the conductor, so the growth is zero.

120
00:06:52,517 --> 00:06:55,703
The inner strings pick up only the last few percent.

121
00:06:55,803 --> 00:06:59,180
The thirteen and three eighths goes from two point two,

122
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:02,467
to two point three meganewtons of added compression.

123
00:07:02,567 --> 00:07:07,144
The nine and five eighths, from one point five one, to one point five six.

124
00:07:07,683 --> 00:07:09,707
The big change is in the foundation.

125
00:07:09,807 --> 00:07:13,954
The conductor and the surface casing can no longer expand at the seabed,

126
00:07:14,054 --> 00:07:16,963
so their own thermal strain turns into compression.

127
00:07:17,063 --> 00:07:20,621
About one hundred and forty four megapascals in the conductor,

128
00:07:20,721 --> 00:07:23,334
and one hundred and eighty in the twenty inch.

129
00:07:23,434 --> 00:07:27,700
That is almost half of its yield strength, before any other load is added.

130
00:07:28,217 --> 00:07:33,088
And the restraint itself must push down with about nineteen and a half meganewtons.

131
00:07:33,188 --> 00:07:36,082
That is the weight of roughly two thousand tonnes.

132
00:07:36,182 --> 00:07:38,358
Even a partial restraint is expensive.

133
00:07:38,458 --> 00:07:42,790
Cutting the growth in half still takes about eight and a half meganewtons.

134
00:07:43,317 --> 00:07:46,325
Restricting growth does not remove the expansion.

135
00:07:46,425 --> 00:07:48,609
It converts displacement into force.

136
00:07:49,950 --> 00:07:51,564
So, does the casing buckle?

137
00:07:51,664 --> 00:07:54,358
To answer that, we need the effective force.

138
00:07:54,458 --> 00:07:57,724
For a pipe filled with fluid and surrounded by fluid,

139
00:07:57,824 --> 00:08:01,661
buckling is not governed by the true axial force in the steel.

140
00:08:01,761 --> 00:08:04,074
It is governed by the effective force.

141
00:08:04,174 --> 00:08:08,328
The axial force, minus the internal pressure times the inside area,

142
00:08:08,428 --> 00:08:11,503
plus the external pressure times the outside area.

143
00:08:11,603 --> 00:08:13,154
This is Lubinski's result.

144
00:08:14,483 --> 00:08:18,475
Before production, the thirteen and three eighths hangs in tension.

145
00:08:18,575 --> 00:08:23,482
The effective force is largest at the wellhead, about one point three meganewtons,

146
00:08:23,582 --> 00:08:26,291
and falls to almost zero at the top of cement,

147
00:08:26,391 --> 00:08:30,198
because each point only carries the weight of the pipe below it.

148
00:08:30,717 --> 00:08:34,590
Heating adds a uniform compression along the whole free length,

149
00:08:34,690 --> 00:08:36,420
so the entire profile shifts.

150
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:40,583
For the restrained well, it shifts by two point three meganewtons.

151
00:08:40,683 --> 00:08:45,818
Now the effective force is compressive all the way from the top of cement up to the

152
00:08:45,918 --> 00:08:46,386
wellhead.

153
00:08:46,917 --> 00:08:50,045
In a vertical well, the critical buckling load is tiny.

154
00:08:50,145 --> 00:08:54,857
Under two hundred kilonewtons for this pipe, because the pipe is long and slender,

155
00:08:54,957 --> 00:08:58,144
and there is no low side for gravity to hold it against.

156
00:08:58,244 --> 00:09:00,139
So the compressed section buckles.

157
00:09:00,239 --> 00:09:01,959
But it cannot fly out sideways.

158
00:09:02,059 --> 00:09:03,661
The outer casing confines it.

159
00:09:03,761 --> 00:09:06,830
It first snakes sinusoidally, then wraps into a helix,

160
00:09:06,930 --> 00:09:08,650
pressed against the outer wall.

161
00:09:09,183 --> 00:09:11,947
The nine and five eighths behaves the same way.

162
00:09:12,047 --> 00:09:16,396
But it carries more hanging weight, so only its lower part is compressed,

163
00:09:16,496 --> 00:09:19,809
from about six hundred metres down to the top of cement.

164
00:09:19,909 --> 00:09:24,623
Restraining the wellhead moves its neutral point about a hundred metres higher.

165
00:09:25,950 --> 00:09:30,024
Here is the thirteen and three eighths inside the twenty inch casing,

166
00:09:30,124 --> 00:09:32,202
with the sideways scale exaggerated.

167
00:09:32,302 --> 00:09:35,106
The radial clearance is sixty eight millimetres.

168
00:09:35,683 --> 00:09:38,683
As the compression grows, the helix tightens.

169
00:09:38,783 --> 00:09:43,230
Lubinski's pitch is pi, times the square root of eight E I over F.

170
00:09:43,330 --> 00:09:46,329
About thirty six metres at the top of cement.

171
00:09:46,883 --> 00:09:50,040
Bending the pipe into a helix adds bending stress.

172
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:53,493
D, times the clearance, times the force, over four I.

173
00:09:53,593 --> 00:09:56,684
Here that is up to about fifty seven megapascals.

174
00:09:56,784 --> 00:09:59,420
The pipe also presses on the outer casing,

175
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:03,068
with a contact force of about two kilonewtons per metre.

176
00:10:03,168 --> 00:10:07,432
That wears both strings whenever something rotates or moves inside.

177
00:10:07,983 --> 00:10:10,020
Buckling gives back a little length,

178
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:12,870
because a helix is shorter than a straight pipe.

179
00:10:12,970 --> 00:10:16,728
But only about five centimetres of the one point one four metres.

180
00:10:16,828 --> 00:10:18,984
So buckling does not relieve the load.

181
00:10:20,350 --> 00:10:21,378
Now add it all up.

182
00:10:21,478 --> 00:10:26,517
In the thirteen and three eighths, axial compression, plus bending, plus pressure,

183
00:10:26,617 --> 00:10:30,841
gives a von Mises stress of about two hundred and thirty megapascals.

184
00:10:30,941 --> 00:10:33,849
That is forty two percent of its yield strength.

185
00:10:33,949 --> 00:10:37,296
The nine and five eighths reaches about twenty percent.

186
00:10:37,817 --> 00:10:38,159
So yes.

187
00:10:38,259 --> 00:10:41,130
The casings buckle, helically, and elastically.

188
00:10:41,230 --> 00:10:44,038
And they would do so with a free wellhead too.

189
00:10:44,138 --> 00:10:46,757
The restraint makes it only slightly worse.

190
00:10:47,283 --> 00:10:52,155
What still needs checking are the connections, which must be rated for compression.

191
00:10:52,255 --> 00:10:57,126
The casing hanger lockdown, which now sees about one meganewton pushing up from the

192
00:10:57,226 --> 00:10:58,744
thirteen and three eighths.

193
00:10:58,844 --> 00:11:00,481
Wear from the contact forces.

194
00:11:00,581 --> 00:11:05,033
And above all, the outer strings and the restraint, which take the real hit.

195
00:11:06,350 --> 00:11:11,068
There is one situation where restraint changes everything for a casing string.

196
00:11:11,168 --> 00:11:14,219
When the twenty inch is not cemented to the seabed.

197
00:11:14,319 --> 00:11:17,246
Say its top of cement is two hundred metres down.

198
00:11:17,346 --> 00:11:21,323
Now the twenty inch has its own free length, inside the conductor.

199
00:11:21,850 --> 00:11:24,462
With a free wellhead, the conductor gives way,

200
00:11:24,562 --> 00:11:28,648
and the twenty inch picks up two and a half meganewtons of compression.

201
00:11:28,748 --> 00:11:31,772
Restrained, that jumps to four point two meganewtons.

202
00:11:32,317 --> 00:11:36,020
It buckles into a fifty three metre helix inside the conductor,

203
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:38,979
with about seventy megapascals of bending stress,

204
00:11:39,079 --> 00:11:42,119
and a combined stress of sixty two percent of yield.

205
00:11:43,450 --> 00:11:43,962
To sum up.

206
00:11:44,062 --> 00:11:46,897
Production heat makes every casing want to grow.

207
00:11:46,997 --> 00:11:51,546
The free lengths are fixed at both ends, so that growth becomes compression,

208
00:11:51,646 --> 00:11:54,237
about two and a half megapascals per degree.

209
00:11:54,783 --> 00:11:58,881
In a subsea well, the inner strings are already almost fully restrained.

210
00:11:58,981 --> 00:12:03,721
They buckle helically in their free sections, whether or not the wellhead can move.

211
00:12:03,821 --> 00:12:07,044
Restraining the wellhead adds only a few percent to them.

212
00:12:07,583 --> 00:12:11,776
The real cost of restraint lands on the conductor, the surface casing,

213
00:12:11,876 --> 00:12:14,657
the wellhead housing, and the restraint itself.

214
00:12:14,757 --> 00:12:15,884
Tens of meganewtons.

215
00:12:15,984 --> 00:12:19,869
If growth must be limited, reduce the heat with insulated tubing,

216
00:12:19,969 --> 00:12:24,897
make sure the surface casing is cemented to the seabed, and design the foundation,

217
00:12:24,997 --> 00:12:29,190
the hanger lockdowns and the connections for the full restrained load.

218
00:12:29,717 --> 00:12:31,731
Thermal expansion is not optional.

219
00:12:31,831 --> 00:12:34,280
Stop the movement, and you get the force.

220
00:12:40,117 --> 00:12:40,681
Part two.

221
00:12:40,781 --> 00:12:43,413
Free versus restrained, side by side.

222
00:12:45,950 --> 00:12:46,491
Same well.

223
00:12:46,591 --> 00:12:47,517
Same production.

224
00:12:47,617 --> 00:12:48,158
Same heat.

225
00:12:48,258 --> 00:12:51,812
The only difference is one boundary condition at the top.

226
00:12:51,912 --> 00:12:54,953
On the left, the wellhead is free to grow upward.

227
00:12:55,053 --> 00:12:59,761
On the right, the wellhead and the conductor are held, so they cannot move.

228
00:13:00,283 --> 00:13:02,638
Let's compare the two cases side by side.

229
00:13:02,738 --> 00:13:07,067
The heat up, the forces on the wellhead, the conductor and surface casing,

230
00:13:07,167 --> 00:13:09,282
the inner casings and their buckling.

231
00:13:09,382 --> 00:13:12,395
And let's find out where the difference really lies.

232
00:13:13,717 --> 00:13:14,756
Production starts.

233
00:13:14,856 --> 00:13:19,882
Oil at about one hundred and eight degrees warms the casings from the inside out,

234
00:13:19,982 --> 00:13:22,224
and every string wants to get longer.

235
00:13:22,324 --> 00:13:26,844
The free sections of the inner casings want to grow by one to two metres.

236
00:13:27,383 --> 00:13:29,365
On the left, the wellhead rises.

237
00:13:29,465 --> 00:13:34,502
After one day it has grown about thirty millimetres, after a month about fifty,

238
00:13:34,602 --> 00:13:39,705
and after a year about sixty one, as the conductor and the surface casing slowly

239
00:13:39,805 --> 00:13:40,226
warm up.

240
00:13:40,750 --> 00:13:42,440
On the right, nothing moves.

241
00:13:42,540 --> 00:13:45,188
Instead, the force on the restraint climbs.

242
00:13:45,288 --> 00:13:49,151
About twelve meganewtons after a day, seventeen after a month,

243
00:13:49,251 --> 00:13:51,516
and nineteen and a half after a year.

244
00:13:51,616 --> 00:13:55,863
The same thermal expansion, showing up as force instead of movement.

245
00:13:57,783 --> 00:13:59,153
The principle is simple.

246
00:13:59,253 --> 00:14:03,500
A heated steel member that is free to move gets longer, with no stress.

247
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:06,440
A member that cannot move stays the same length,

248
00:14:06,540 --> 00:14:11,154
and instead carries about two and a half megapascals of compression for every

249
00:14:11,254 --> 00:14:11,583
degree.

250
00:14:11,683 --> 00:14:14,645
Movement and force are two sides of the same coin.

251
00:14:15,883 --> 00:14:18,968
The well is a set of springs tied to one wellhead.

252
00:14:19,068 --> 00:14:21,898
The long, free inner casings are soft springs.

253
00:14:21,998 --> 00:14:26,612
The conductor and the cemented surface casing, gripped by cement and soil,

254
00:14:26,712 --> 00:14:30,561
form one very stiff spring, more than a hundred times stiffer.

255
00:14:31,083 --> 00:14:33,892
That stiffness ratio decides almost everything.

256
00:14:33,992 --> 00:14:36,058
Because the foundation is so stiff,

257
00:14:36,158 --> 00:14:40,638
the inner strings cannot push the wellhead very far, even when it is free.

258
00:14:40,738 --> 00:14:45,651
They are already held back, and carry ninety to ninety six percent of their fully

259
00:14:45,751 --> 00:14:46,642
restrained load.

260
00:14:48,583 --> 00:14:51,677
Now look at the forces acting on the wellhead itself.

261
00:14:51,777 --> 00:14:56,317
With a free wellhead, the hot inner strings push up with about four point one

262
00:14:56,417 --> 00:14:57,040
meganewtons.

263
00:14:57,140 --> 00:15:01,921
The wellhead rises until the conductor and surface casing are stretched enough to

264
00:15:02,021 --> 00:15:04,271
pull back with the same four point one.

265
00:15:04,371 --> 00:15:05,596
The net force is zero.

266
00:15:06,117 --> 00:15:10,310
With the wellhead restrained, the inner strings push up slightly harder,

267
00:15:10,410 --> 00:15:12,040
four point three meganewtons.

268
00:15:12,140 --> 00:15:15,678
But now the conductor and the surface casing push up as well,

269
00:15:15,778 --> 00:15:20,210
with about fifteen meganewtons, because their own expansion at the seabed is

270
00:15:20,310 --> 00:15:20,687
blocked.

271
00:15:20,787 --> 00:15:22,774
The restraint has to react the sum.

272
00:15:22,874 --> 00:15:27,247
Nineteen and a half meganewtons, roughly the weight of two thousand tonnes.

273
00:15:28,583 --> 00:15:31,056
This is where the two cases really differ.

274
00:15:31,156 --> 00:15:35,160
Here is the axial force along the conductor and the surface casing,

275
00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:36,997
in the top two hundred metres.

276
00:15:37,097 --> 00:15:39,814
Tension to the right, compression to the left.

277
00:15:40,350 --> 00:15:40,572
Free.

278
00:15:40,672 --> 00:15:44,371
At the seabed, the inner strings pull the composite upward,

279
00:15:44,471 --> 00:15:45,981
so the top is in tension.

280
00:15:46,081 --> 00:15:49,330
A few tens of metres down, soil friction takes over,

281
00:15:49,430 --> 00:15:53,322
and the casing's own thermal expansion turns into compression.

282
00:15:53,850 --> 00:15:54,417
Restrained.

283
00:15:54,517 --> 00:15:59,269
The top is locked, so the full thermal compression reaches all the way up to the

284
00:15:59,369 --> 00:15:59,815
wellhead.

285
00:15:59,915 --> 00:16:02,241
About fifteen meganewtons at the seabed.

286
00:16:02,783 --> 00:16:06,418
Below about a hundred metres, the two curves are identical.

287
00:16:06,518 --> 00:16:11,101
Cement and rock already hold the surface casing completely, in both cases.

288
00:16:11,201 --> 00:16:14,393
The whole difference sits in the top hundred metres,

289
00:16:14,493 --> 00:16:17,241
where the soft soil lets the conductor slide.

290
00:16:17,783 --> 00:16:22,284
In the steel, that is a change of about one hundred and ninety megapascals at the

291
00:16:22,384 --> 00:16:22,682
seabed.

292
00:16:22,782 --> 00:16:26,942
From plus forty four, to minus one hundred and forty four in the conductor,

293
00:16:27,042 --> 00:16:31,031
and from plus eight, to minus one hundred and eighty in the twenty inch.

294
00:16:31,131 --> 00:16:35,064
Just below the conductor shoe, where the conductor hands its load over,

295
00:16:35,164 --> 00:16:39,438
the twenty inch locally reaches about three hundred megapascals in our model.

296
00:16:39,538 --> 00:16:41,199
Around eighty percent of yield.

297
00:16:42,517 --> 00:16:43,727
Now the inner casings.

298
00:16:43,827 --> 00:16:48,491
The thirteen and three eighths picks up two point two meganewtons of compression

299
00:16:48,591 --> 00:16:51,945
with a free wellhead, and two point three when restrained.

300
00:16:52,045 --> 00:16:53,315
Only five percent more.

301
00:16:53,850 --> 00:16:58,985
In both cases its effective force is compressive along the entire thirteen hundred

302
00:16:59,085 --> 00:17:00,134
metre free length.

303
00:17:00,234 --> 00:17:04,539
So it is helically buckled in both cases, with almost the same pitch,

304
00:17:04,639 --> 00:17:06,646
bending stress and contact force.

305
00:17:07,183 --> 00:17:11,711
For the nine and five eighths, the buckled zone starts at six hundred and ninety

306
00:17:11,811 --> 00:17:16,107
metres with a free wellhead, and at five hundred and ninety four metres when

307
00:17:16,207 --> 00:17:16,744
restrained.

308
00:17:16,844 --> 00:17:21,198
About a hundred metres more buckled pipe, for three percent more compression.

309
00:17:21,717 --> 00:17:22,435
Why so small?

310
00:17:22,535 --> 00:17:26,022
When free, the wellhead moves only sixty one millimetres.

311
00:17:26,122 --> 00:17:30,239
Compared with the one to two metres the inner strings want to grow,

312
00:17:30,339 --> 00:17:31,812
that is almost no relief.

313
00:17:31,912 --> 00:17:35,652
Restraining the wellhead takes away a relief they hardly had.

314
00:17:36,983 --> 00:17:38,645
Here is the full comparison.

315
00:17:39,183 --> 00:17:41,914
For the inner casings, the numbers barely move.

316
00:17:42,014 --> 00:17:44,323
Compression rises three to five percent,

317
00:17:44,423 --> 00:17:49,322
the highest combined stress in the thirteen and three eighths goes from two hundred

318
00:17:49,422 --> 00:17:53,839
and seventeen to two hundred and twenty nine megapascals, well below yield,

319
00:17:53,939 --> 00:17:57,934
and the uplift on its hanger from zero point nine to one meganewton.

320
00:17:58,483 --> 00:18:01,581
The rows that change dramatically are at the bottom.

321
00:18:01,681 --> 00:18:05,701
The conductor and surface casing stresses, and the restraint force,

322
00:18:05,801 --> 00:18:09,145
which goes from zero to nineteen and a half meganewtons.

323
00:18:11,483 --> 00:18:14,116
In practice, no restraint is perfectly rigid.

324
00:18:14,216 --> 00:18:18,368
Any real restraint lands somewhere on this line between the two cases.

325
00:18:18,468 --> 00:18:22,923
Halving the growth to twenty eight millimetres still costs eight and a half

326
00:18:23,023 --> 00:18:23,652
meganewtons.

327
00:18:23,752 --> 00:18:27,721
Roughly a third of a meganewton for every millimetre you take away.

328
00:18:28,250 --> 00:18:29,842
And the load comes and goes.

329
00:18:29,942 --> 00:18:33,710
Every shut in cools the well, and every start up heats it again.

330
00:18:33,810 --> 00:18:36,188
A free wellhead simply moves up and down.

331
00:18:36,288 --> 00:18:39,633
A restrained one cycles its full force, on the restraint,

332
00:18:39,733 --> 00:18:43,561
the conductor and the wellhead housing, for the life of the well.

333
00:18:45,483 --> 00:18:49,865
There is one situation where the comparison changes for a casing string.

334
00:18:49,965 --> 00:18:53,102
A surface casing that is not cemented to the seabed.

335
00:18:53,202 --> 00:18:57,210
Then the twenty inch has its own free length inside the conductor,

336
00:18:57,310 --> 00:18:59,326
and the foundation is much softer.

337
00:18:59,850 --> 00:19:02,504
Free, the wellhead grows seventy millimetres,

338
00:19:02,604 --> 00:19:05,870
and the twenty inch carries two and a half meganewtons.

339
00:19:05,970 --> 00:19:08,380
Restrained, that jumps to four point two.

340
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:12,603
Sixty eight percent more, and it buckles harder inside the conductor.

341
00:19:12,703 --> 00:19:14,745
Here, the restraint really matters.

342
00:19:16,683 --> 00:19:18,480
So what is the real difference?

343
00:19:18,580 --> 00:19:20,621
For the inner casings, very little.

344
00:19:20,721 --> 00:19:22,823
They buckle helically in both cases,

345
00:19:22,923 --> 00:19:25,881
and the restraint adds only three to five percent.

346
00:19:26,417 --> 00:19:28,638
For the conductor, the surface casing,

347
00:19:28,738 --> 00:19:31,448
the wellhead housing and the restraint itself,

348
00:19:31,548 --> 00:19:35,664
it is the difference between almost no load, and tens of meganewtons,

349
00:19:35,764 --> 00:19:37,374
cycling with every start up.

350
00:19:37,917 --> 00:19:41,348
Free or restrained, the thermal expansion is the same.

351
00:19:41,448 --> 00:19:45,534
The only choice is whether it shows up as movement, or as force.

352
00:19:50,683 --> 00:19:51,296
Part three.

353
00:19:51,396 --> 00:19:52,269
Your questions.

354
00:19:57,817 --> 00:19:58,511
Question one.

355
00:19:58,611 --> 00:20:01,996
How much of the restraint force is in the surface casing,

356
00:20:02,096 --> 00:20:03,830
and how much in the conductor?

357
00:20:03,930 --> 00:20:06,276
And why do they create such high forces?

358
00:20:07,717 --> 00:20:09,743
Of the nineteen and a half meganewtons,

359
00:20:09,843 --> 00:20:12,959
about eight and a half come from the thirty inch conductor,

360
00:20:13,059 --> 00:20:15,794
four and a half from the twenty inch surface casing,

361
00:20:15,894 --> 00:20:18,738
and about two from the cement between and around them.

362
00:20:18,838 --> 00:20:21,354
The inner strings add the last four point three.

363
00:20:21,454 --> 00:20:24,571
So the conductor and the surface casing, with their cement,

364
00:20:24,671 --> 00:20:26,588
give almost four fifths of the total.

365
00:20:27,117 --> 00:20:27,736
Why so high?

366
00:20:27,836 --> 00:20:32,534
Steel that cannot expand carries about two and a half megapascals of compression

367
00:20:32,634 --> 00:20:34,693
for every degree, whatever its size.

368
00:20:34,793 --> 00:20:39,130
Just below the seabed the conductor has warmed by about sixty one degrees,

369
00:20:39,230 --> 00:20:41,649
and the twenty inch by about seventy five.

370
00:20:41,749 --> 00:20:44,947
Fully blocked, that is about one hundred and fifty six,

371
00:20:45,047 --> 00:20:47,526
and one hundred and ninety two megapascals.

372
00:20:48,050 --> 00:20:49,892
Force is stress times steel area.

373
00:20:49,992 --> 00:20:52,834
The conductor, thirty inches with a one inch wall,

374
00:20:52,934 --> 00:20:56,483
has five hundred and eighty eight square centimetres of steel.

375
00:20:56,583 --> 00:21:00,072
That is four point four times the thirteen and three eighths.

376
00:21:00,172 --> 00:21:04,603
So every degree costs one hundred and fifty one kilonewtons in the conductor,

377
00:21:04,703 --> 00:21:09,134
sixty four in the twenty inch, and only thirty four in the thirteen and three

378
00:21:09,234 --> 00:21:09,605
eighths.

379
00:21:10,150 --> 00:21:11,980
And these strings cannot escape.

380
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:16,263
Cement and soil hold them sideways, so they cannot buckle to shed load,

381
00:21:16,363 --> 00:21:18,012
and they are extremely stiff.

382
00:21:18,112 --> 00:21:22,536
Every millimetre of growth you prevent costs about a third of a meganewton.

383
00:21:23,883 --> 00:21:24,546
Question two.

384
00:21:24,646 --> 00:21:25,836
Doesn't length matter?

385
00:21:25,936 --> 00:21:29,647
With a longer pipe, surely you need to restrain a longer section?

386
00:21:31,083 --> 00:21:33,109
For the force, surprisingly, no.

387
00:21:33,209 --> 00:21:37,492
A longer pipe does want to grow more, in proportion to its length.

388
00:21:37,592 --> 00:21:42,009
But it is also a softer spring, in inverse proportion to its length.

389
00:21:42,109 --> 00:21:44,732
Multiply the two, and the length cancels.

390
00:21:44,832 --> 00:21:48,916
The force to hold it is E, times A, times alpha, times delta T.

391
00:21:49,450 --> 00:21:50,679
Think of it per metre.

392
00:21:50,779 --> 00:21:55,210
Every metre of hot steel needs the same stress to cancel its own expansion.

393
00:21:55,310 --> 00:21:57,567
That stress acts on each cross section.

394
00:21:57,667 --> 00:22:00,890
It does not add up along the pipe, the way weight does.

395
00:22:00,990 --> 00:22:04,273
A thirteen hundred metre, a six hundred and fifty metre,

396
00:22:04,373 --> 00:22:09,227
and a ten metre piece of the same pipe all need two point four meganewtons to hold

397
00:22:09,327 --> 00:22:09,529
them.

398
00:22:10,050 --> 00:22:12,950
Your intuition is right about other things, though.

399
00:22:13,050 --> 00:22:16,656
A longer pipe needs more movement prevented, and in the ground,

400
00:22:16,756 --> 00:22:20,067
the force has to be anchored by friction over some length.

401
00:22:20,167 --> 00:22:24,832
The conductor needs roughly the top sixty to a hundred metres of soil to hold it.

402
00:22:24,932 --> 00:22:27,949
Below that, cement and rock lock every metre locally,

403
00:22:28,049 --> 00:22:31,714
so a longer conductor would not raise the force at the wellhead.

404
00:22:33,050 --> 00:22:33,756
Question three.

405
00:22:33,856 --> 00:22:37,730
What are the buckling limit and the yield limit for the different casings?

406
00:22:39,150 --> 00:22:42,995
For the inner casings in a vertical hole, the buckling limit is tiny.

407
00:22:43,095 --> 00:22:47,284
Helical buckling starts at about one hundred and eighty kilonewtons for the

408
00:22:47,384 --> 00:22:51,000
thirteen and three eighths, eighty for the nine and five eighths,

409
00:22:51,100 --> 00:22:52,487
and twenty for the tubing.

410
00:22:52,587 --> 00:22:56,318
Their thermal loads are ten to twenty times higher, so they buckle.

411
00:22:56,418 --> 00:22:59,005
In a deviated section the limit is much higher,

412
00:22:59,105 --> 00:23:01,922
about one and a half meganewtons at thirty degrees.

413
00:23:02,450 --> 00:23:04,021
But buckling is not failure.

414
00:23:04,121 --> 00:23:07,185
The outer casing confines the pipe to a stable helix.

415
00:23:07,285 --> 00:23:12,200
The real limit for a buckled string is yield, from compression plus helical bending.

416
00:23:12,300 --> 00:23:16,677
That happens at about five point two meganewtons for the thirteen and three

417
00:23:16,777 --> 00:23:20,020
eighths, five point eight for the nine and five eighths,

418
00:23:20,120 --> 00:23:21,990
and one point one for the tubing.

419
00:23:22,090 --> 00:23:24,616
Roughly two to three times the actual loads.

420
00:23:25,150 --> 00:23:30,023
In pure compression, the pipe body yields at seven point four meganewtons for the

421
00:23:30,123 --> 00:23:34,567
thirteen and three eighths, seven point six for the nine and five eighths,

422
00:23:34,667 --> 00:23:39,601
nine and a half for the twenty inch, and twenty two point seven for the conductor.

423
00:23:39,701 --> 00:23:42,549
A fully blocked pipe, starting from zero stress,

424
00:23:42,649 --> 00:23:47,522
would only yield after about one hundred and fifty degrees of heating for K fifty

425
00:23:47,622 --> 00:23:51,144
five and X fifty six, two hundred and fifteen for L eighty,

426
00:23:51,244 --> 00:23:53,969
and two hundred and ninety five for P one ten.

427
00:23:54,517 --> 00:23:58,253
The conductor and the cemented twenty inch do not buckle at all.

428
00:23:58,353 --> 00:24:00,352
Soil and cement hold them sideways,

429
00:24:00,452 --> 00:24:05,028
which puts their buckling load at sixty to two hundred and eighty meganewtons.

430
00:24:05,128 --> 00:24:06,766
For them, yield is the limit.

431
00:24:06,866 --> 00:24:10,783
The closest point is the twenty inch just below the conductor shoe.

432
00:24:10,883 --> 00:24:14,680
About three hundred and eight megapascals in the restrained case,

433
00:24:14,780 --> 00:24:16,358
eighty one percent of yield.

434
00:24:17,683 --> 00:24:18,387
Question four.

435
00:24:18,487 --> 00:24:21,603
You said the inner casings are already mostly locked in.

436
00:24:21,703 --> 00:24:23,153
What does that really mean?

437
00:24:24,583 --> 00:24:27,036
Both ends of each free section are fixed.

438
00:24:27,136 --> 00:24:28,966
The bottom is bonded in cement.

439
00:24:29,066 --> 00:24:34,009
The top hangs from a hanger that sits on a load shoulder in the wellhead housing,

440
00:24:34,109 --> 00:24:35,814
held down by a lockdown ring.

441
00:24:35,914 --> 00:24:39,114
So the top can only move if the whole wellhead moves.

442
00:24:39,650 --> 00:24:41,036
That leaves two ways out.

443
00:24:41,136 --> 00:24:44,068
The wellhead can rise and carry the hanger with it,

444
00:24:44,168 --> 00:24:48,408
and the pipe can coil into a helix, which is slightly shorter end to end.

445
00:24:48,508 --> 00:24:53,163
Whatever growth is left over is blocked, and blocked growth becomes compression.

446
00:24:53,683 --> 00:24:56,664
Here is the budget for the thirteen and three eighths.

447
00:24:56,764 --> 00:25:00,658
It wants to grow about one thousand one hundred and forty millimetres.

448
00:25:00,758 --> 00:25:04,253
The rising wellhead takes sixty one, and the helix forty eight.

449
00:25:04,353 --> 00:25:07,163
More than a thousand millimetres are still blocked.

450
00:25:07,263 --> 00:25:10,187
That is ninety percent of the fully restrained force.

451
00:25:10,287 --> 00:25:13,325
For the nine and five eighths it is ninety six percent,

452
00:25:13,425 --> 00:25:15,151
and for the tubing ninety three.

453
00:25:16,383 --> 00:25:18,426
Why does the wellhead move so little?

454
00:25:18,526 --> 00:25:21,206
It sits on the conductor and the surface casing,

455
00:25:21,306 --> 00:25:25,839
which behave like a spring of about two hundred and forty meganewtons per metre.

456
00:25:25,939 --> 00:25:29,720
The inner strings are springs of two meganewtons per metre or less.

457
00:25:29,820 --> 00:25:33,948
Their four meganewton push lifts the wellhead only seventeen millimetres.

458
00:25:34,048 --> 00:25:37,481
The other forty four come from the conductor's own expansion.

459
00:25:37,581 --> 00:25:40,260
It is a tug of war the inner strings cannot win.

460
00:25:41,483 --> 00:25:43,378
And this is where length matters.

461
00:25:43,478 --> 00:25:48,336
The same sixty one millimetres would relieve a hundred metre free section by about

462
00:25:48,436 --> 00:25:52,386
seventy percent, but a thirteen hundred metre section by only five.

463
00:25:52,486 --> 00:25:55,832
Long free lengths are locked, whatever the wellhead does.

464
00:25:56,350 --> 00:26:00,398
So the inner strings buckle whether or not the wellhead is restrained.

465
00:26:00,498 --> 00:26:03,479
The only real unlock would be the hanger sliding up,

466
00:26:03,579 --> 00:26:05,435
and that would be a seal failure.

467
00:26:05,535 --> 00:26:09,465
What actually reduces their load is less heat, more hanging tension,

468
00:26:09,565 --> 00:26:11,479
and stronger pipe and connections.

469
00:26:12,817 --> 00:26:15,758
Free or restrained, the expansion is the same.

470
00:26:15,858 --> 00:26:18,204
It shows up as movement, or as force.

471
00:26:18,304 --> 00:26:22,567
And in a subsea well, most of that force ends up in the conductor,

472
00:26:22,667 --> 00:26:25,806
the surface casing, and whatever holds them down.
